<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kathiawar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saurashtra peninsula, Saurashtra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kathiawar, also called Saurashtra, is a large peninsula of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, bounded by the Gulf of Kutch and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>. Named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathi-people">Kathi people</a>, it was divided in the colonial period among some two hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> supervised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar-agency">Kathiawar Agency</a>. It includes Somnath, Dwarka, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a> hills and the Gir forest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kathiawar.jpg" alt="Kathiawar" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Worldview with sea borders from Open Street Map · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saurashtra_Gujarat_India_Satellite_Map_3_February_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beyond this core the emperors ruled through feudatory dynasties, the samantas, who governed their own lands, paid tribute and sent troops; among them were the Chahamanas of Shakambhari, the Guhilas, the Chandela dynasty, the Kalachuris and the Chalukyas of <b>Saurashtra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ptolemies had built the Red Sea ports of Myos Hormos and Berenike for voyages to the Indus delta, <b>Kathiawar</b> and Muziris; Eudoxus of Cyzicus sailed to India about 130 BCE and returned with perfumes and gemstones, and by the time Indo-Greek rule was ending up to 120 ships…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture formed the backbone of the economy, relying on the fertile Ganges basin and on irrigation works such as the Sudarshana reservoir in <b>Saurashtra</b>, attributed to Chandragupta's governor, to grow rice, wheat, barley and pulses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most scholars place its origin in a western dialect rather than an eastern one: it shares features with the Junagadh rock inscription of Rudradaman in <b>Kathiawar</b>, and A. K. Warder links it with Avanti, where the Sthavira Nikāya was centred, though it keeps some eastern traits called Māgadhisms.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Amarnagar, earlier known as Thana Amarnagar, was a small estate in <b>Kathiawar</b> held by a local chiefly family from around 1700.</blockquote><a href="/state/amarnagar-state">Amarnagar State</a></li><li><blockquote>Bajana lay on the southern edge of the Little Rann of Kutch in the Jhalawad region of <b>Kathiawar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bajana-state">Bajana State</a></li></ul></section>
